Raise the DTLS receive buffer from 4 KiB to 16 KiB, which is what actually caps
the usable SCTP path MTU.

`DtlsTransport::doRecv()` declares a fixed stack receive buffer -- separately in
each of the three TLS branches, all at 4096 -- and then, once the handshake
finishes, hands *the same number* to the TLS implementation as its MTU:

    const size_t bufferSize = 4096;
    byte buffer[bufferSize];
    ...
    SSL_set_mtu(mSsl, bufferSize + 1);        // DTLS is told its MTU is 4097
    ...
    ret = SSL_read(mSsl, buffer, bufferSize); // a record must fit in 4096

So it is not merely a buffer size. An SCTP chunk plus DTLS overhead (~42 bytes of
header and AEAD tag) has to fit in 4096, which puts the usable ceiling on
`rtc::Configuration::mtu` at roughly 4150 -- and A11 measured exactly that edge
before this patch existed: an MTU of 4132 works and 4256 does not.

**The failure above the edge is silent, which is why this is worth patching rather
than documenting.** A 64-byte message is one small record and keeps flowing at full
rate, while every message that needs a full-size chunk cannot be read at all. From
OpenSSL's point of view nothing is wrong, so nothing logs it: the transport
handshakes, carries small traffic, and stalls on payload.

A11 raises the MTU because usrsctp implements no path MTU discovery, so
libdatachannel falls back to RFC 8261's safe 1280 and fragments every message to
1172-byte chunks whatever the path can carry. A11's own RFC 8899 search
(`WebRtcConfiguration::path_mtu_discovery`, default on) probes for the real limit
and settled at 4132 on both reference machines -- not because that is the path's
limit, but because it is the largest value whose records fit this buffer. Its
`max_discovered_mtu` is already 9216, so the search has headroom the moment the
buffer does.

16 KiB rather than something larger because these are stack arrays in a function
running on libdatachannel's own `ThreadPool` threads, and because 16 KiB covers
every MTU worth having: jumbo frames are 9216, and loopback is 16384 on macOS.

The constant is hoisted to file scope so the three branches cannot drift apart.
Only the OpenSSL branch is compiled and measured in this tree; the GnuTLS and
MbedTLS branches carry the identical constant and the identical cliff, so they are
moved with it.

